THE PORT/C MEMORY RATIO IS A FUNCTION OF PRESET, AND THE PORT'S WORST PRESET IS
THE FASTEST ONE — 0.50x C at p6 and 0.94x at p13, on the same cell.

date:   2026-09-03
host:   r7900x — AMD Ryzen 7900X, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu. HEAP is heaptrack;
        RSS is `/usr/bin/time -v` max RSS (median of 5 at p13, 3 at p6).
        Harness `rust/tools/mem_peak.sh`.
trees:  base  = 01ef6f1f1 (main)
        after = base + the one-byte `MeCandidate` commit + the `perf_encode`
                one-copy harness commit
data:   benchmarks/mem_preset_2026-09-03.tsv

=================== WHY THIS FILE EXISTS =====================================

Every memory record in this repo before today measured ONE preset, and the two
that measured different ones (p6 in `benchmarks/mem_arms_2026-09-02.*`, p13 in
the `mem_heaptrack_*` series) were never put side by side. They disagree, and
the reason is not noise: **C's footprint moves enormously with preset and the
port's barely moves at all.** On gradient 2048x2048 qp 40:

  peak heap, 2048x2048 inter arm      p6        p13     p6/p13
    port (base)                    146.04 M   139.62 M   1.05x
    C                              240.25 M   120.12 M   2.00x

C doubles between p13 and p6; the port grows 5 %. So the port/C ratio at a
FAST preset is the port's worst case, and any conclusion drawn from one preset
is a conclusion about that preset only.

=================== PORT/C, BOTH PRESETS, AFTER THE TWO CHANGES ==============

  peak HEAP port/C            512    1024    1280    1536    1920    2048
    p6  still                0.321   0.511      -    0.617      -    0.614
    p6  videokey             0.271   0.381      -    0.439      -    0.429
    p6  inter                0.359   0.444      -    0.480      -    0.496
    p13 still                    -       -   0.605   0.622   0.640   0.644
    p13 videokey                 -       -   0.692   0.708   0.723   0.730
    p13 inter                    -       -   0.886   0.910   0.933   0.938

  peak RSS port/C             512    1024    1280    1536    1920    2048
    p6  still                0.725   0.815      -    0.890      -    0.902
    p6  videokey             0.584   0.640      -    0.669      -    0.675
    p6  inter                0.630   0.685      -    0.725      -    0.749
    p13 still                    -       -   0.905   0.929   0.958   0.969
    p13 videokey                 -       -   0.913   0.940   0.954   0.950
    p13 inter                    -       -   1.035   1.048   1.122   1.086

**Every one of the 24 cells is inside the 25 % goal on both metrics, and only
three cells exceed 1.0 at all** — the p13 inter arm at 1280/1536/1920/2048,
1.035x to 1.122x.

=================== THE SAME GRID ON `main` ==================================

  peak RSS port/C on 01ef6f1f1          p6 2048   p13 2048
    still                                0.956     1.045
    videokey                             0.752     1.090
    inter                                0.839     1.331
  (each divided by the C column of its OWN run — C was measured twice at p13
   and the two runs agree within 0.6 %, which is the control on the spread.)

So the p13 inter arm was the only place the goal was in doubt, and it was in
doubt by 33 %. It now reads 1.086x. The p6 arm was never outside the goal at
all on this harness — which is worth stating plainly, because
`benchmarks/mem_arms_2026-09-02.meta` records the inter arm at **1.60x** at
4 MP and p6. That number is aarch64, `capture_c_trace` on the C side, and a
different day; it does not reproduce here at 0.84x, and NOTHING in this file
explains the difference. Do not treat either as refuting the other until the
same harness has been run on both hosts — the change of ISA, of C driver, and
of code are all confounded.

=================== NOT ESTABLISHED ==========================================
  * Any aarch64 number. x86_64-linux only.
  * Any preset other than 6 and 13. Presets 0-5 and 7-12 are NOT measured, and
    the two measured points do not establish the shape between them.
  * Any content but gradient, any qp but 40, any size outside 512-2048.
  * Byte identity on the inter arm at these sizes: C's `.obu` differs from the
    port's on every inter cell here (the inter byte frontier is 64/128/256
    square), so those rows compare two encoders doing SIMILAR work. The still
    and videokey arms ARE byte-identical at every size and preset in the grid
    except p6 2048 videokey (107,177 vs 107,368).
  * Repeat variance on the heap half: one encode per cell.
